a gender perspective.

00:17:18.904 --> 00:17:22.241
I decided that the problems
are not just in one state.

00:17:22.241 --> 00:17:24.877
They seem to be in many states.

00:17:24.877 --> 00:17:28.347
So one of the things that
makes common sense to me

00:17:28.347 --> 00:17:33.085
is the parent is the person
who should be in the biggest,

00:17:33.085 --> 00:17:35.521
most powerful position possible

00:17:35.521 --> 00:17:37.990 align:start size:84% position:16%
in making decisions
for their kids.

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:40.793
Big labor unions and bureaucrats

00:17:40.793 --> 00:17:43.962
are in the way of those parents

00:17:43.962 --> 00:17:48.333
making the decisions and then
working with their teachers

00:17:48.333 --> 00:17:50.002
to carry that out.

00:17:50.002 --> 00:17:52.371
I want that to be nationwide,

00:17:52.371 --> 00:17:55.874 align:start size:88% position:13%
not in one or two states
where the governors

00:17:55.874 --> 00:17:59.445 align:start size:97% position:3%
are taking that issue
seriously, every single state.

00:17:59.445 --> 00:18:01.747
When you're talking
about a 12-year-old,

00:18:01.747 --> 00:18:04.483
the parents should be engaged

00:18:04.483 --> 00:18:07.119
in the decisions of their kids.

00:18:07.119 --> 00:18:10.656
That's just common sense in
my house, where I grew up.

00:18:10.656 --> 00:18:12.858 align:start size:97% position:3%
I want it to be common sense
all across the country.

00:18:12.858 --> 00:18:15.694 align:start size:97% position:3%
- When you talk to the
Democrats in South Carolina

00:18:15.694 --> 00:18:17.996
